<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Lullubi</h1><p class="st-meta">2400 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A people and kingdom of the central Zagros, immortalised as the defeated enemy on Naram-Sin's victory stele and on the rock reliefs of Sar-e Pol-e Zahab.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lullubi lived in the mountains around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor">Shahrizor</a> plain and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala</a>, on the borders of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. They first appear in the late third millennium BCE as opponents of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> defeated their ruler Satuni around 2250 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Lullubi were organised in highland chiefdoms that at times united under kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin's</a> stele, later carried off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, shows him trampling Lullubi warriors on a mountain slope. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sar-e-pol-e-zahab">Sar-e Pol-e Zahab</a> a Lullubi king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anubanini">Anubanini</a>, carved his own relief with the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>, imitating Akkadian royal art. Lowland rulers prized their lands for livestock and slaves.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Lullubi remained a frontier people for over a millennium, campaigned against by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a>, Hurrian rulers and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>. By the 9th century BCE their land was called <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zamua">Zamua</a>, and they fade from the record by the 7th century, absorbed into Assyrian provinces and later the Median world.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lullubi.jpg" alt="Lullubi" width="320" height="255"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elam1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2400 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Satuni, Anubanini</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lullubum, Lulubi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1525&amp;focus=lullubi" aria-label="Lullubi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5807 972 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lullubi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6127 1228h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6127 1228h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-1525&amp;focus=lullubi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

